== Chapter XI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The Third Direction proposed. Load the Conscience with the Guilt of the perplexing Distemper. The Ways and Means whereby that may be done. The Fourth Direction. Vehement desire for Deliverance. The Fifth. Some Distempers rooted deeply in men's Natural Tempers. Considerations of such Distempers: Ways of dealing with them. The Sixth Direction. Occasions and Advantages of Sin to be prevented. The Seventh Direction. The first actings of Sin vigorously to be opposed.]]
]

==== Third Direction: Load the Conscience with Its Guilt

#smallcaps[THIS] is my #emph[Third] Direction:

#emph[Load thy Conscience with the Guilt of it.] Not only consider, that it hath a Guilt, but load thy Conscience with the Guilt of its actual Eruptions and Disturbances.

For the right improvement of this #emph[Rule,] I shall give some particular #emph[Directions.]

First, Take Gods #emph[Method] in it, and begin with #emph[Generals,] and so descend to #emph[particulars.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) Charge thy #emph[Conscience] with that Guilt which appears in it, from the #emph[Rectitude] and #emph[Holiness] of the Law. Bring the #emph[holy Law] of God into thy #emph[Conscience]\; lay thy #emph[corruption] to it; pray that thou mayest be affected with it. Consider the #emph[holiness, spirituality, fiery severity, inwardness, absoluteness] of the #emph[Law]\; And see how thou canst stand before it. Be much (I say) in affecting thy #emph[Conscience] with the Terror of the Lord in the #emph[Law,] and how #emph[righteous] it is that every #emph[one] of thy Transgressions should receive a recompense of Reward. Perhaps thy #emph[Conscience] will invent #emph[shifts] and Evasions to keep off the Power of this Consideration; as, that the #emph[condemning power] of the #emph[Law] doth not belong to thee, thou art set free from it, and the like; and so though thou be not #emph[conformable] to it, yet thou needest not to be so much troubled at it. But,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Tell thy #emph[Conscience,] that it cannot manage any evidence to the purpose, that thou art free from the #emph[condemning] Power of Sin, whilst thy #emph[unmortified] Lust lies in thy Heart; so that perhaps the #emph[Law] may make good its Plea against thee for a #emph[full Dominion,] and then thou art a lost Creature. Wherefore it is best to ponder to the utmost, what it hath to say.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Assuredly he that pleads in the most secret Reserve of his Heart, that he is freed from the #emph[condemning power of the Law,] thereby secretly to countenance himself in giving the least #emph[allowance] unto any Sin or Lust, is not able on Gospel grounds to manage any Evidence unto any tolerable spiritual Security, that indeed he is in a due manner freed from what he so pretends himself to be delivered.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Whatever be the #emph[issue,] yet the #emph[Law] hath Commission from God to seize upon Transgressors wherever it find them, and so bring them before his Throne, where they are to plead for themselves; This is thy present case: The Law hath found thee out, and before God it will bring thee: If thou canst plead a Pardon, well and good; If not, the Law will do its work.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. However, this is the #emph[proper Work] of the #emph[Law,] to discover Sin in the Guilt of it, to awake and humble the Soul for it, to be a Glass to represent Sin in its colors; and if thou denyest to deal with it on this Account, it is not through #emph[Faith,] but through the #emph[hardness] of thy #emph[Heart] and the Deceitfulness of Sin.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is a Door that too many #emph[Professors] have gone out at, unto open #emph[Apostasy]\; such a Deliverance from the Law they have pretended, as that they would consult its Guidance and Direction no more; they would measure their Sin by it no more; by little and little this Principle hath insensibly from the #emph[Notion] of it proceeded to influence their #emph[practical] Understandings; and having taken possession there, hath turned the Will and Affections loose to all manner of Abominations.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[By such ways (I say then) as these, persuade thy Conscience to hearken #emph[diligently] to what the Law speaks in the Name of the Lord unto thee, about thy Lust and Corruption. Oh! if thy Ears be open, it will speak with a #emph[Voice] that shall make thee #emph[tremble,] that shall cast thee to the #emph[ground,] and fill thee with #emph[Astonishment.] If ever thou wilt #emph[mortify] thy #emph[Corruptions,] thou must tie up thy #emph[Conscience] to the #emph[Law,] shut it from all #emph[shifts] and Exceptions until it owns its #emph[Guilt,] with a clear and thorough Apprehension: So that thence, (as #emph[David] speaks) thy #emph[Iniquity may ever be before thee.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) Bring thy #emph[Lust] to the #emph[Gospel,] not for Relief, but for farther #emph[conviction] of its Guilt; look on him whom thou hast pierced, and be in bitterness. Say to thy Soul; What have I done? What #emph[Love,] what #emph[Mercy,] what #emph[Blood,] what #emph[Grace] have I #emph[despised] and trampled on? Is this the Return I make to the Father for his #emph[Love,] to the Son for his #emph[Blood,] to the Holy Ghost for his #emph[Grace]? Do I thus requite the Lord? Have I defiled the #emph[Heart] that Christ died to wash; that the Blessed Spirit hath chosen to dwell in? And can I keep myself out of the Dust? What can I say to the dear Lord Jesus? How shall I hold up my head with any boldness before him? Do I account #emph[Communion] with him of so little value, that for this #emph[vile Lusts] sake I have scarce left him any room in my Heart? How shall I escape, if I neglect so great Salvation? In the mean time, what shall I say to the Lord? #emph[Love, Mercy, Grace,] Goodness, Peace, Joy, Consolation, I have despised them all, and esteemed them as a thing of #emph[nought,] that I might #emph[harbor] a Lust in my Heart.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Have I obtained a view of Gods Fatherly Countenance, that I might behold his #emph[face,] and provoke him to his #emph[face?] Was my Soul washed, that #emph[room] might be made for new Defilements? Shall I endeavor to disappoint the End of the Death of #emph[Christ]? Shall I daily grieve that Spirit whereby I am sealed to the day of Redemption? Entertain thy Conscience daily with this #emph[Treaty.] See if it can stand before this #emph[Aggravation] of its Guilt. If this make it not sink in some measure, and melt, I fear thy Case is dangerous.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, Descend to #emph[particulars.] As under the #emph[General] Head of the Gospel, all the Benefits of it are to be considered, as #emph[Redemption, Justification] and the like; so in #emph[particular,] consider the Management of the love of them toward thine own Soul, for the Aggravation of the Guilt of thy Corruption. As,]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Consider the #emph[infinite] Patience and #emph[forbearance] of God towards #emph[thee] in particular: Consider what Advantages he might have taken against thee, to have made thee a #emph[shame] and a #emph[reproach] in this World, and an #emph[object] of wrath for ever: How thou hast dealt treacherously and falsely with him from time to time, #emph[flattered] him with thy Lips, but broken all Promises and #emph[Engagements]\; and that by the means of that Sin thou art now in pursuit of; and yet he hath spared thee from time to time, although thou seemest #emph[boldly] to have put it to the trial how long he could hold out: And wilt thou yet sin against him? Wilt thou yet weary him, and make him to serve with thy Corruptions?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Hast thou not often been ready to conclude thyself, that it was utterly impossible that he should bear any #emph[longer] with thee; that he would cast thee off, and be gracious no more; that all his Forbearance was exhausted, and #emph[Hell] and Wrath was even ready prepared for thee; and yet above all thy Expectation he hath returned with Visitations of Love; and wilt thou yet abide in the Provocation of the eyes of his Glory?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. How often hast thou been at the #emph[door] of being Hardened by the Deceitfulness of Sin; and by the #emph[infinite rich Grace] of God hast been recovered to #emph[communion] with him again?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Hast thou not found Grace #emph[decaying]\; Delight in Duties, Ordinances, Prayer and Meditation, #emph[vanishing]\; inclinations to loose careless walking, thriving; and they who before were entangled, almost beyond recovery? Hast thou not found thyself engaged in such Ways, Societies, Companies, and that with delight, as God abhors? And wilt thou venture any more to the brink of Hardness?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. All Gods gracious dealings with thee in Providential #emph[Dispensations,] Deliverances, Afflictions, Mercies, Enjoyments, all ought here to take place. By these I say, and the like #emph[Means, load] thy #emph[Conscience,] and leave it not until it be #emph[thoroughly] affected with the #emph[Guilt] of thy indwelling Corruption: Until it is sensible of its Wound, and lie in the dust before the Lord. Unless this be done to the purpose, all other Endeavors are to no purpose. Whilst the #emph[Conscience] hath any Means to alleviate the #emph[Guilt] of Sin, the Soul will never vigorously attempt its Mortification.]]

==== Fourth Direction: A Constant Longing for Deliverance

#emph[Fourthly,] Being thus #emph[affected] with thy Sin, in the #emph[next place,] get a #emph[constant longing, breathing after deliverance from the Power of it.] Suffer not thy #emph[Heart] one moment to be #emph[contented] with thy present Frame and Condition. #emph[Longing] desires after any thing, in things #emph[Natural] and #emph[Civil,] are of no #emph[value] nor consideration, any farther, but as they incite and #emph[stir] up the #emph[person] in whom they are, to a diligent #emph[use] of Means for the bringing about the thing aimed at. In spiritual things it is otherwise. #emph[Longing,] breathing and panting after Deliverance, is a #emph[Grace] in itself, that hath a #emph[mighty] power to #emph[conform] the Soul into the likeness of the thing longed after. Hence the #emph[Apostle] describing the #emph[Repentance] and #emph[godly] Sorrow of the #emph[Corinthians,] reckons this as one eminent #emph[Grace] that was then set on work; #emph[vehement Desire,] 2 Cor 7:11. And in this case of indwelling Sin, and the #emph[power] of it, what Frame doth he express #emph[himself to be in?] Rom 7:24. His #emph[heart] breaks out with longings into a most #emph[passionate Expression] of desire of deliverance. Now if this be the #emph[frame] of Saints, upon the #emph[general consideration] of indwelling Sin, how is it to be #emph[heightened] and increased, when #emph[thereunto] is added the #emph[perplexing] Rage and Power of any #emph[particular Lust] and Corruption? Assure thyself, unless thou #emph[longest] for Deliverance thou shalt not have it.

This will make the Heart #emph[watchful] for all Opportunities of Advantage against its Enemy; and ready to close with #emph[any Assistances] that are afforded for its Destruction; strong Desires are the very Life of that #emph[praying always] which is enjoined us in all Conditions, and in none is more necessary than in this; they set Faith and Hope on work, and are the Souls #emph[moving] after the Lord.

Get thy Heart then into a #emph[panting] and #emph[breathing] Frame, #emph[long, sigh,] cry out; you know the Example of #emph[David,] I shall not need to insist on it.

==== Fifth Direction: Consider Whether It Is Rooted in the Nature

The #emph[Fifth Direction] is,

5#emph[ly,] Consider whether the #emph[Distemper] with which thou art perplexed, be not rooted in thy #emph[Nature,] and cherished, fomented and #emph[heightened] from thy #emph[Constitution.] A proneness to some Sins may doubtless lie in the Natural Temper and Disposition of men. In this Case consider;

1\. This is not in the least an #emph[Extenuation] of the #emph[Guilt] of thy Sin.

Some with an #emph[open] Profaneness will ascribe #emph[gross] Enormities to their #emph[Temper] and Disposition. And whether others may not #emph[relieve] themselves from the #emph[pressing] Guilt of their #emph[Distempers] by the same Consideration, I know not. It is from the #emph[Fall,] from the #emph[Original] depravation of our Natures, that the #emph[fomes] and Nourishment of any Sin abides in our Natural Temper. #emph[David] reckons his being #emph[shapen in Iniquity,] and #emph[conception in Sin,] Ps 51:5 as an Aggravation of his following Sin, not a #emph[lessening] or extenuation of it. That thou art #emph[peculiarly] inclined unto any sinful Distemper, is but a peculiar breaking out of #emph[Original] Lust in thy #emph[Nature,] which should peculiarly #emph[abase] and #emph[humble] thee.

2\. That thou hast to #emph[fix] upon on this account, in reference to thy #emph[walking] with God, is, that so great an Advantage is given to Sin, as also to Satan, by this thy Temper and Disposition, that without extraordinary Watchfulness, Care and Diligence, they will #emph[assuredly] prevail against thy Soul. Thousands have been on this account hurried headlong to #emph[Hell,] who otherwise (at least) might have gone at a more #emph[gentle,] less #emph[provoking,] less #emph[mischievous] rate.

3\. For the Mortification of any Distemper, so #emph[rooted] in the Nature of a Man, unto all other Ways and Means already named or farther to be #emph[insisted] on, there is one #emph[expedient] peculiarly suited. This is that of the Apostle, 1 Cor 9:27. #emph[I keep under my Body, and bring it into subjection.] The #emph[bringing] of the very #emph[Body] into subjection, is an #emph[Ordinance] of God, tending to the #emph[Mortification] of Sin. This gives #emph[check] unto the Natural #emph[Root] of the Distemper, and withers it by taking away its #emph[fatness] of soil. Perhaps because the #emph[Papists] (men ignorant of the #emph[Righteousness] of Christ, the Work of his Spirit, and whole #emph[business] in hand) have laid the #emph[whole] weight and stress of #emph[Mortification] in voluntary #emph[Services] and Penances; leading to the #emph[subjection] of the Body, knowing indeed the true Nature neither of #emph[Sin] nor #emph[Mortification,] it may on the other side be a Temptation to some, to neglect some means of #emph[humiliation,] which by God himself are owned and appointed. The #emph[bringing] of the #emph[body] into subjection in the #emph[case] insisted on, by cutting short the #emph[Natural Appetite,] by #emph[fasting, watching,] and the like, is doubtless #emph[acceptable] to God, so it be done with the ensuing #emph[limitations.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) That the outward #emph[weakening] and impairing of the Body, be not looked upon as a thing good in itself, or that any #emph[Mortification] doth #emph[consist] therein, (which were again to bring us under #emph[carnal Ordinances]) but only as a Means for the End proposed; the #emph[weakening] of any Distemper in its Natural root and seat. A man may have #emph[leanness] of Body and Soul together.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) That the #emph[means] whereby this is done, namely, by #emph[fasting] and #emph[watching,] and the like, be not #emph[looked] on as things that in Themselves, and by virtue of their Own Power, can produce true #emph[Mortification] of any Sin; for if they would, Sin might be mortified without any help of the Spirit, in any #emph[unregenerate] person in the world. They are to be looked on only as ways whereby the #emph[Spirit] may, and sometimes doth put forth #emph[strength] for the accomplishing of his own #emph[work,] especially in the Case mentioned. Want of a right understanding and due Improvement of these and the like Considerations, hath raised a #emph[Mortification] among the Papists that may be better applied to Horses and other Beasts of the Field, than to #emph[Believers.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is the #emph[sum] of what hath been spoken; when the #emph[distemper] complained of, seems to be rooted in Natural temper and #emph[constitution,] in applying our Souls to a participation of the #emph[Blood] and #emph[Spirit] of Christ, an Endeavor is to be used, to give #emph[check] in the way of God, to the natural Root of that Distemper.]]

==== Sixth Direction: Watch Against Its Occasions and Advantages

#emph[Sixthly,] Consider what #emph[Occasions,] what #emph[Advantages] thy Distemper hath taken to exert and put forth itself, and #emph[watch] against them all. This is one part of that Duty which our blessed Savior #emph[recommends] to his Disciples under the name of #emph[watching,] Mark 13:37. #emph[I say unto you all, Watch]\; which in Luke 21:34 is, #emph[Take heed that your hearts be not overcharged:] Watch against #emph[all Eruptions] of thy #emph[Corruptions.] I mean that Duty which #emph[David] professed himself to be exercised unto: #emph[I have] (saith he) #emph[kept myself from mine Iniquity.] he watched all the #emph[ways] and workings of his Iniquity to prevent #emph[them,] to rise up against them. This is that which we are called unto under the name of #emph[Considering] our Ways: Consider what #emph[Ways,] what #emph[Companies,] what #emph[Opportunities,] what #emph[Studies,] what #emph[Businesses,] what #emph[Conditions,] have at any time #emph[given,] or do usually give #emph[advantages] to thy #emph[Distempers,] and set thyself heedfully against them all. Men will do this with respect unto their #emph[bodily] infirmities and distempers; The Seasons, the Diet, the Air, that have proved #emph[offensive] shall be avoided. Are the things of the Soul of less importance? Know that he that dares to #emph[dally] with #emph[Occasions] of Sin, will dare to Sin. He that will venture upon Temptations unto Wickedness, will venture upon Wickedness. #emph[Hazael] thought he should not be so #emph[wicked] as the Prophet told him he would be: To convince him, the Prophet tells him no #emph[more,] but Thou shalt be #emph[King of Syria.] If he will venture on Temptations unto #emph[Cruelty,] he will be #emph[cruel.] Tell a man he shall commit such and such Sins, he will #emph[startle] at it: If you can #emph[convince] him, that he will #emph[venture] on such #emph[Occasions] and Temptations of them, he will have little ground left for his Confidence. Particular Directions belonging to this Head are many, not now to be insisted on. But because this Head is of no less importance than the whole Doctrine here handled, I have at large in another Treatise, about #emph[entering into Temptations,] treated of it.

==== Seventh Direction: Rise Mightily Against Its First Actings

#emph[Seventhly,] Rise #emph[mightily] against the #emph[first Actings] of thy Distemper, its first Conceptions; suffer it not to get the least Ground. Do not say, thus far it shall go, and no farther. If it have #emph[allowance] for #emph[one] step, it will take #emph[another.] It is impossible to fix Bounds to Sin. It is like water in a Channel; if it once break out, it will have its #emph[course.] Its not #emph[acting,] is easier to be compassed than its Bounding. Therefore doth #emph[James] give that #emph[Gradation] and Process of #emph[Lust,] Chapter 1:14, 15 that we may stop at the Entrance. Dost thou find thy #emph[corruption] to begin to entangle thy Thoughts; rise up with all thy strength against it, with no #emph[less Indignation] than if it had fully accomplished what it aims at. Consider what an #emph[unclean] Thought would have; it would have thee #emph[roll] thyself in Folly and Filth. Ask #emph[Envy] what it would have; #emph[Murder] and Destruction is at the end of it. Set thyself against it with no less #emph[vigor,] than if it had utterly #emph[debased] thee to wickedness. Without this Course thou wilt not prevail. As Sin gets #emph[ground] in the Affections to #emph[delight] in it, it gets also upon the Understanding to #emph[slight] it.
